    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> restored order to most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s murder, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> without ever taking the title of shah.</li><li>His mild rule revived trade and farming and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil">Vakil</a> bazaar, mosque and bath, still among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>'s finest 18th-century buildings.</li><li>After his death in 1779 his relatives fought for the throne, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> Agha Mohammad Khan destroyed the dynasty by 1794.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> called himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil-al-ruaya">Vakil al-Ruaya</a>, Deputy of the People, rather than shah, and kept a Safavid prince as nominal sovereign.</p></aside>
    </section>

