    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand.jpg" alt="Karim Khan Zand" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karim_Khan-e_Zand.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zand Empire was established around 1750 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>, chief of the Zand, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lak">Lak</a> tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, who emerged from the power vacuum left by the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747 to become the dominant figure in a country shattered by decades of war and political collapse. Initially allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> chief Ali Mardan Khan, with whom he installed a young Safavid prince as the puppet Shah Ismail III, Karim Khan outmanoeuvred his partner and then defeated his other rivals, including Mohammad Hasan Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azad-khan-afghan">Azad Khan Afghan</a> and the Afsharid claimants. His rise was distinguished from the succession of brutal warlords that followed Nader Shah's death by a combination of military effectiveness and a genuinely humane style of rule that has made him one of the most popular rulers in Iranian memory. Unlike his competitors, Karim Khan refused to take the title of shah, preferring that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil">Vakil</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil-al-ruaya">Vakil al-Ruaya</a> ('Deputy of the People'), a deliberate choice that presented his authority as service to the population rather than dynastic self-aggrandisement. He kept Ismail III as a nominal Safavid sovereign for much of his reign, positioning himself as regent rather than usurper and avoiding open challenge to the prevailing sense of legitimate kingship. His campaigns established Zand control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fars">Fars</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> as his capital from the mid-1760s, and over most of the Iranian heartland, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#isfahan">Isfahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gilan">Gilan</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khorasan">Khorasan</a> remained under Nader Shah's blind grandson Shahrokh at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mashhad">Mashhad</a>. In 1776 his forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basra">Basra</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after a long siege. By his death in 1779, Karim Khan had transformed much of Iran from a battleground of competing warlords into a functioning state with effective administration, reviving commerce and a level of public security not seen since the late Safavid period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shiraz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shiraz.jpg" alt="Shiraz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darvaze_Ghoran11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">آرش (Arche), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zand economy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> was marked by a deliberate revival of commerce and agriculture after the devastation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> period, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> becoming one of the most commercially active cities of the region in the empire's peak decades. Karim Khan actively encouraged trade by moderating taxes on commerce, securing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> and cultivating relations with European trading companies. In 1763 he granted the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a> the right to establish a factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bushire">Bushire</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bushehr">Bushehr</a>) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> maintained a post on Kharg Island until it was lost to the Gulf chieftain Mir Muhanna in 1766. Through the Gulf ports, Indian textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a> and other goods entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kerman">Kerman</a> wool, Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a>, carpets, dried fruit and other agricultural products moved outward through the same networks. Shiraz was rebuilt as the capital with an architectural investment that made it one of the most handsome cities in Iran: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil-bazaar">Vakil Bazaar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil-mosque">Vakil Mosque</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil-bath">Vakil Bath</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arg-of-karim-khan">Arg of Karim Khan</a> remain among the finest examples of eighteenth-century Persian architecture, testifying to the fiscal and commercial vitality of the Zand period at its height. Agricultural recovery across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fars">Fars</a> and neighbouring regions, encouraged by improved security and the return of displaced people, restored much of the productive capacity lost in the preceding decades. The rival Zand princes who followed Karim Khan, a rapid succession of rulers across fifteen years of civil war, progressively consumed the commercial and agricultural capital his rule had accumulated, and by the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a> destroyed the Zand state in 1794, Iran's economy had fallen well below its Karim Khan-era peak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-kerman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-kerman.jpg" alt="Siege of Kerman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-kerman">Siege of Kerman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capture_and_Sack_of_Kerman_by_Agha_Mohammad_Khan_Qajar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zand army was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> tribal force built around the cavalry and infantry of the Zand and allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lak">Lak</a>, Lur and other tribes, supplemented by Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#musket">musketeers</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tofangchi">tofangchi</a>) and artillery that gave it a combined-arms capability greater than that of most of its Iranian rivals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s military success rested on his personal leadership, since his soldiers were loyal to him as an individual rather than to any institutional hierarchy, combined with strategic patience and a willingness to negotiate when negotiation served his purposes. His decision to contain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> chiefs of the north rather than destroy them, holding the young Agha Mohammad Khan as a hostage at his court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a>, proved unwise in retrospect but reflected his preference for accommodation over unlimited warfare. The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basra">Basra</a> (1775–1776), conducted by Karim Khan's brother Sadeq Khan, was the most ambitious Zand military operation: a prolonged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naval-blockade">blockade</a> that eventually compelled the Ottoman garrison to surrender, demonstrating the army's capacity for sustained operations, though Basra was abandoned after Karim Khan's death. The dynasty's military weakness was the reverse of its strength, its dependence on Karim Khan's personal authority. After his death in 1779, succession struggles among his relatives and tribal subordinates consumed the army in fratricidal warfare from which it never recovered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a>, an extraordinarily ruthless commander who had spent some sixteen years as a hostage at the Zand court and harboured deep grievances against the dynasty, exploited this fragmentation with methodical campaigns. In 1791 the governor of Shiraz, Haji Ebrahim Khan Kalantar, betrayed the last Zand ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>, and handed the city to the Qajars; in 1794, after the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-kerman">Siege of Kerman</a>, Agha Mohammad Khan took the city, and Lotf Ali Khan, betrayed as he fled at Bam, was captured, tortured and put to death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shah.jpg" alt="Nader Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Nader_Shah._Artist_unknown,_created_in_ca._1740_in_Iran_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zand government bore the strong personal imprint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>, whose accessible and comparatively restrained style of rule created an administrative atmosphere that contemporaries found remarkably benign by the standards of eighteenth-century Iranian politics. He is reported to have dressed simply, to have been personally accessible to petitioners seeking redress, and to have exercised a degree of personal oversight of justice that reduced, though it did not eliminate, the corruption endemic in Persian provincial administration. The administrative structure beneath him combined Safavid institutions, which Zand rule consciously sought to revive, with the pragmatic tribal arrangements through which the Zand had always operated, and officials such as the kalantar (mayor) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> handled much of the day-to-day business of government. Provincial government was entrusted to appointees who were typically Zand kinsmen or trusted tribal allies rather than career officials of the idealised Safavid kind, creating personal loyalties that served adequately during Karim Khan's lifetime but could not survive the succession crisis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam">Shia</a> clerical establishment was treated with considerable respect: Karim Khan supported the construction and upkeep of religious buildings, endowed shrines, and avoided the theological controversies, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s attempt to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiism">Shi'ism</a> recognised as a fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> school, that had soured his predecessor's relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulama">ulama</a>. This clerical goodwill gave the Zand regime a degree of religious legitimacy that its lack of Safavid lineage would otherwise have denied it. Justice was administered through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sharia">sharia</a> courts for personal status and many criminal matters, through the customary jurisdiction of state officials, and through tribal adjudication for disputes within the Zand tribal network.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-ocean.jpg" alt="Indian Ocean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Ocean-CIA_WFB_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">United States Central Intelligence Agency, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zand Empire's wider historical significance was limited by its brief duration and regional scope, but its impact on the subsequent development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> was substantial and complex. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s revival of commerce and his cultivation of relations with European trading companies set precedents for Iranian engagement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> trading system that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a> would inherit. The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s establishment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bushehr">Bushehr</a>, founded with Zand permission, later became the seat of the British Residency in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, the foundation of the British commercial and political influence in the Gulf that shaped Iranian foreign relations throughout the nineteenth century. Architecturally, Karim Khan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> is one of the most complete surviving ensembles of eighteenth-century Iranian urban building, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vakil">Vakil</a> complex of bazaar, mosque and bath remains the outstanding expression of the Zand style, notable for its painted tilework, carved stone and use of floral and figurative motifs. The Zand period's patronage of literature and the arts helped preserve the continuity of Persian cultural traditions through the chaos of the post-Nader era: Shiraz remained an active centre of poetry, painting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, and Karim Khan restored the tomb of Hafez in 1773, while the oil paintings and lacquerwork of the Zand court foreshadowed the Qajar court style that followed. The empire's demonstration that Iran could recover a measure of commercial and administrative functionality within a generation of the Afsharid wars showed the resilience of Iranian urban and commercial traditions and provided an institutional foundation, however damaged by the subsequent civil wars, on which Qajar government was built. Regionally, the relative peace and commercial revival of the Zand period allowed a degree of demographic recovery and agricultural reconstruction that partly reversed the depopulation caused by decades of war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#lotf-ali-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan.jpg" alt="Lotf Ali Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lotfali_khan_zand2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nimazandyf, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zand Empire's decline began immediately upon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s death in March 1779 and unfolded with a rapidity that showed how completely the state had been a personal rather than an institutional creation. Karim Khan died without securing the succession: his half-brother Zaki Khan seized power in the name of Karim Khan's sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-al-fath-khan-zand">Abu al-Fath Khan</a> and Mohammad Ali Khan but was murdered within months, inaugurating fifteen years of civil war in which the throne passed in rapid succession between Sadeq Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ali-murad-khan-zand">Ali Murad Khan</a>, Ja'far Khan and others, several of whom met violent deaths. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> chief Agha Mohammad Khan, who had been held as a hostage at the Zand court for years and who had been castrated as a boy on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s nephew Adel Shah, escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> on Karim Khan's death and began building Qajar power in the north, making Tehran his base. His campaigns were marked by extraordinary ruthlessness: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kerman">Kerman</a> fell in 1794, he reportedly ordered some 20,000 of its inhabitants blinded in revenge for the city's support of the Zand, the most notorious atrocity of a violent era. The last Zand ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>, brave, handsome and militarily gifted but lacking the political base to restore the dynasty, fought a remarkable series of campaigns from 1789 to 1794 that kept Zand resistance alive against worsening odds. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-capture-of-shiraz">Qajar capture of Shiraz</a> in 1791, betrayed to them by its governor, cost him his capital, and after his defence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-kerman">final siege at Kerman</a> failed he was betrayed at Bam, captured, tortured and executed by Agha Mohammad Khan in 1794, completing the Zand Empire's dissolution. Agha Mohammad Khan was crowned shah in 1796, founding the Qajar dynasty that would rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> until 1925.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Zand Empire's supreme historical significance lies in the anomaly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>, a ruler who refused the title of king, governed with well-attested humanity, and created a brief era of commercial and cultural revival between two periods of catastrophic violence, demonstrating that even in the most brutal political environments the character of an individual ruler can profoundly shape the experience of an entire society.</em></p>

